@module projects-m01 | Real-World Projects
@summary Fifteen guided apps from a counter to a production-style application.

@lesson projects-m01-l01 | Counter Application | 45
## Learning objectives
- Build a complete counter application
- Practise architecture, state and testing

## Counter Application
Build the classic counter with increment, decrement and reset, using setState.

## Dependencies
- Flutter SDK only

## Steps
1. Create the project
2. Add a StatefulWidget holding the count
3. Add three buttons
4. Prevent negative values

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class CounterApp extends StatefulWidget {
  const CounterApp({super.key});
  @override
  State<CounterApp> createState() => _CounterAppState();
}

class _CounterAppState extends State<CounterApp> {
  int count = 0;
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Scaffold(
        body: Center(child: Text('$count', style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.displayLarge)),
        floatingActionButton: Row(mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.end, children: [
          FloatingActionButton(onPressed: count > 0 ? () => setState(() => count--) : null, child: const Icon(Icons.remove)),
          const SizedBox(width: 8),
          FloatingActionButton(onPressed: () => setState(() => count++), child: const Icon(Icons.add)),
        ]),
      );
}
```

## Testing
Write a unit test for the core logic and a widget test that taps the main action and checks the result.

## Summary
- You built a working counter application.
- Extend it with your own features.

@lesson projects-m01-l02 | Calculator Application | 45
## Learning objectives
- Build a complete calculator application
- Practise architecture, state and testing

## Calculator Application
Build a calculator with a grid of buttons and correct operator handling.

## Dependencies
- Flutter SDK only

## Steps
1. Lay out a GridView of buttons
2. Store the expression in state
3. Evaluate left to right with + − × ÷
4. Handle division by zero

```dart
double calculate(double a, String op, double b) => switch (op) {
      '+' => a + b,
      '-' => a - b,
      '×' => a * b,
      '÷' => b == 0 ? double.nan : a / b,
      _ => throw ArgumentError(op),
    };
void main() => print(calculate(6, '÷', 3));
```

## Testing
Write a unit test for the core logic and a widget test that taps the main action and checks the result.

## Summary
- You built a working calculator application.
- Extend it with your own features.

@lesson projects-m01-l03 | Todo Application | 45
## Learning objectives
- Build a complete todo application
